subcode:: sections
    $call section, reference, Reference
    $p
        This chapter collects the attributes, directives, and tools of the earlier chapters in one place, as tables to look things up in. Each entry was checked against the source; the source repository remains the final word.

    $call subsection, ref_page, Page attributes
    $p
        Lines of the form $(code:name&#58; value) right below a $(code:page&#58;) line, before any other code, are page attributes (see $(a:Collisions with target-language syntax,#syntax_clash) for what that means for output text of the same form). They can also be given in the $(code:config) file, where they apply to every page. These are the ones read by $(code:MyDef) itself and its tools:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Attribute"
            $th "Read by"
            $th "Meaning"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:module)
            $td
                all tools
            $td
                output module for the page, e.g. $(code:module&#58; perl). See $(a:mydef_page,#mydef_page) for how it combines with $(code:-m) and $(code:config).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:output_dir)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_page), $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                where the output file goes; a relative path is appended to the $(code:-o) or $(code:config) directory
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:type)
            $td
                all modules
            $td
                extension of the output file: $(code:type&#58; md) writes $(code:notes.md), and an empty $(code:type&#58;) writes a file without an extension. A page name with an extension, as in $(code:page&#58; index.html), works too.
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:package)
            $td
                $(code:output_perl), $(code:output_java)
            $td
                Perl: the package name, and the page becomes a $(code:.pm) module. Java: adds a $(code:package) line.
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:autolist)
            $td
                output modules
            $td
                which $(code:fncode) functions are included even when nothing calls them. $(code:output_perl): any value includes all of them (set automatically for a $(code:.pm)). $(code:output_c): $(code:global) includes the top-level ones, $(code:page) the ones defined inside the page.
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:relax)
            $td
                $(code:output_perl)
            $td
                leaves out $(code:use strict;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:subpage)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_page)
            $td
                the page is compiled but not written
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:skiprun)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                do not run this page (in a $(code:.def) file with several pages)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:arg), $(code:args)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                command-line arguments for the program: $(code:arg&#58; P1 P2)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:cmd)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                the command to run instead of the default one: $(code:cmd&#58; cat t.txt)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:run)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                a prefix for the run command: $(code:run&#58; time) runs $(code:time perl t.pl), or $(code:time ./t) for a compiled program
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:exe)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                the command that runs a compiled program, instead of $(code:./t); $(code:exe&#58; none) only compiles
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:exe_type)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                an extension for the compiled program: $(code:exe_type&#58; exe) builds $(code:t.exe)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:CC), $(code:CFLAGS)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run), $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                the C compiler command and its flags: $(code:CC&#58; gcc -O0)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:lib_list)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run)
            $td
                libraries for the link line: $(code:lib_list&#58; -lm)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:make_dep)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                extra dependencies for the page's rule in the $(code:Makefile)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:make_cmd)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                the command for the page's rule, instead of $(code:mydef_page)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:LIB), $(code:other)
            $td
                $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                for C projects, $(code:mydef_make) also writes a $(code:Makefile) in each output directory: $(code:LIB) sets its libraries, and $(code:other) makes the page one of its targets
    $p
        Output modules and libraries read attributes of their own, such as $(code:title&#58;) in $(code:output_www) and $(code:size&#58;) in $(code:java/gui.def); see their chapters. The second field of the page line, as in $(code:page&#58; t&#44; basic_frame), names the frame (see the $(a:glossary,#ref_glossary)).

    $call subsection, ref_directive, Compile-time directives
    $p
        Directives in $(code:&dollar;&#40;...&#41;) are handled while the page is compiled; none of them reach the output. Three kinds of them are written on a line of their own and take the indented block below them:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Directive"
            $th "Does"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;if&#58;cond&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;elif&#58;cond&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;else&#41;)
            $td
                compile the block when the condition holds. The block ends with the indentation; there is no $(code:&dollar;&#40;endif&#41;). Conditions are listed in $(a:Preprocessing: if,#preproc_if).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;for&#58;x in a&#44; b&#44; c&#41;)
            $td
                compile the block once for each item, with $(code:&dollar;&#40;x&#41;) set. Ranges such as $(code:1-3) and $(code:a-z) work. See $(a:Preprocessing: for,#preproc_for).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;for&#58;x&#44; i in a&#44; b and 1&#44; 2&#41;)
            $td
                loop over several lists together
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;for&#58;a&#44; b and x&#44; y&#41;)
            $td
                the same without names: $(code:&dollar;1), $(code:&dollar;2) in the block
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;block&#58;name&#41;)
            $td
                collect the block into the named block $(code:name), which a $(code:DUMP_STUB name) line elsewhere puts out
    $p
        Others set macros. They are written on a line of their own too, and have no block:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Directive"
            $th "Does"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;set&#58;name=value&#41;)
            $td
                set a macro in the current scope. See $(a:Preprocessing: set,#preproc_set).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;set&#58;n+=1&#41;)
            $td
                update a number with $(code:+=), $(code:-=), $(code:*=), or $(code:/=); $(code:.=) appends text
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;set&#58;L[&#44; ]=item&#41;)
            $td
                append $(code:item) to $(code:L), with $(code:&#44; ) between the items
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;set-1&#58;name=value&#41;)
            $td
                set it one scope up, in the code that called the subcode; $(code:&dollar;&#40;export&#58;name=value&#41;) is the same
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;setmacro&#58;name=value&#41;)
            $td
                set it globally, as if under $(code:macros&#58;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;setpage&#58;name=value&#41;)
            $td
                set it for the rest of the page
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;reset&#58;name=value&#41;)
            $td
                change the macro in the scope where it is defined
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;mset&#58;a=1&#44; b=2&#41;)
            $td
                set several macros in the current scope; $(code:&dollar;&#40;mexport&#58;...&#41;) sets them one scope up
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;unset&#58;a&#44; b&#41;)
            $td
                hide macros from the current scope down
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;eval&#58;name=expr&#41;)
            $td
                set $(code:name) to the value of a Perl expression: $(code:&dollar;&#40;eval&#58;E=&dollar;&#40;N&#41;*2&#41;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;split&#58;name&#41;)
            $td
                split a comma-separated macro into $(code:&dollar;&#40;p_n&#41;) (the count) and $(code:&dollar;&#40;p_1&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;p_2&#41;), ...
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;assert&#58;cond&#41;)
            $td
                warn when the condition is false
    $p
        The rest are expanded in place, anywhere in a line:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Expansion"
            $th "Gives"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#41;)
            $td
                the value of the macro
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;a&#44; b&#41;)
            $td
                the value, with $(code:&dollar;1) and $(code:&dollar;2) in it replaced by $(code:a) and $(code:b). See $(a:Inline macros with parameters,#macro_param).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;6&#58;&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;0&#58;5&#41;)
            $td
                a substring: from position 6 on, or 5 characters from position 0
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;0&#58;word&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;0&#58;number&#41;)
            $td
                the first word or number from that position
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;lc&#41;), $(code:&#117;c), $(code:&#117;cfirst), $(code:&#108;ength)
            $td
                the Perl function of the value
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;strip&#41;)
            $td
                the value without its first and last character, e.g. the quotes
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;regex&#58;pat&#41;)
            $td
                the first capture group of $(code:pat) in the value
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;list&#58;n&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;list&#58;1&#41;)
            $td
                the number of items in a comma-separated value, or the item at an index, counting from 0
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#58;quote&#108;ist&#41;)
            $td
                the items in double quotes: $(code:&quot;a&quot;&#44; &quot;b&quot;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;x3&#58;ab&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;x3&#44;&#58;ab&#41;)
            $td
                $(code:ab) repeated: $(code:ababab), or with a separator: $(code:ab&#44;ab&#44;ab)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;join&#58;v&dollar;1&#58; + &#58;1-3&#41;)
            $td
                a pattern joined over a list: $(code:v1 + v2 + v3)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;subst&#58;name&#58;pat&#58;rep&#41;)
            $td
                the value with the regex $(code:pat) replaced
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;eval&#58;expr&#41;)
            $td
                the value of a Perl expression: $(code:&dollar;&#40;eval&#58;ucfirst&#40;&quot;&dollar;&#40;name&#41;&quot;&#41;&#41;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;sym&#58;C++&#41;)
            $td
                a symbol name for a string: $(code:CPlusPlus)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;&#40;def&#58;date&#41;), $(code:&dollar;&#40;def&#58;datetime&#41;)
            $td
                the modification date of the $(code:.def) file
    $p
        For more on these, see $(a:Special macro syntax,#macro_special).

    $call subsection, ref_keyword, Keywords in the output stream
    $p
        A few words written alone on a line are instructions to the output stage rather than output. Subcodes and output modules use them, and page code can too:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Keyword"
            $th "Does"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:BLOCK)
            $td
                in a subcode called with $(code:&amp;call), where the caller's block goes. See $(a:Block call,#block_call).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:DUMP_STUB name)
            $td
                puts out the named block $(code:name), which can be filled later. See $(a:DUMP_STUB,#syntax_dump_stub).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:INDENT), $(code:DEDENT)
            $td
                indent the following lines one more or one less level
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:PUSHDENT), $(code:POPDENT)
            $td
                start at the left margin, and return to the saved indentation
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:SOURCE_INDENT), $(code:SOURCE_DEDENT)
            $td
                the same as $(code:INDENT) and $(code:DEDENT)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:NEWLINE)
            $td
                an empty line
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:NEWLINE?)
            $td
                an empty line, unless the previous line is already empty
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:NOOP)
            $td
                nothing; a placeholder line
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:INCLUDE_FILE path)
            $td
                the contents of a file, as they are
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&lt;-|text)
            $td
                $(code:text) at the left margin, whatever the indentation
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: general
            start
            INDENT
            indented
            DEDENT
            back
            <-|left margin
    &call codeprint
        start
            indented
        back
        left margin

    $call subsection, ref_tools, Tools
    $p
        The $(a:Invoking MyDef,#invoke) chapter covers $(code:mydef_page), $(code:mydef_make), and $(code:mydef_run) in detail. In short:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Tool"
            $th "Does"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_page [-mmodule] [-odir] t.def)
            $td
                compiles the pages of a $(code:.def) file
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_make)
            $td
                writes a $(code:Makefile) for the $(code:.def) files in a directory tree (and a $(code:config) the first time)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_run [-mmodule] [-silent] t.def)
            $td
                compiles, then compiles and runs the output with the tool its extension calls for. It also runs a plain source file, e.g. $(code:mydef_run t.c).
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_test)
            $td
                runs $(code:mydef_run) on each $(code:.def) file listed in a $(code:TESTS) file and reports the failures
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_install [-f] src dst ext)
            $td
                installs files, only those newer than the installed copy ($(code:-f) installs them all)
    $p
        $(code:mydef_test) checks a test with an expect block at the end of its $(code:.def) file. $(code:/* expect output&#58;) compares what the program prints; with any other word, as in $(code:/* expect t.txt&#58;), it compares the generated file. Blank lines and runs of spaces are ignored in the comparison, and a mismatch prints $(code:not ok) and fails the test:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: perl
            $print hello

        \x2f* expect output:
        hello
        */
    &call codeprint, sh
        $ cat TESTS
        t.def
        $ mydef_test
        *** [ t.def ] ***
        PAGE: t
          --> [./t.pl]
        perl ./t.pl
        hello
        --------------------------
            ok 1/1
        Ran 1 tests.
    $p
        $(code:mydef_install) is what the $(code:make install) of $(code:MyDef) and its module repositories runs. The extension picks the destination:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Command"
            $th "Installs"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_install deflib . def)
            $td
                the $(code:.def) files under $(code:deflib) into $(code:&dollar;MYDEFLIB)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_install out/lib . pm)
            $td
                the $(code:.pm) files under $(code:out/lib) into $(code:&dollar;PERL5LIB)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:mydef_install - file ...)
            $td
                programs into $(code:&dollar;MYDEFBIN), or $(code:~/bin) when it is on the $(code:PATH)

    $call subsection, ref_glossary, Glossary
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Term"
            $th "Meaning"
        $tr
            $td
                page
            $td
                a $(code:page&#58; name) block; each page compiles to one output file. See $(a:Basic structure of .def file,#syntax_defile).
        $tr
            $td
                module
            $td
                the output module that translates a page for one target language, chosen with $(code:module&#58;), e.g. $(code:output_perl) for $(code:module&#58; perl)
        $tr
            $td
                subcode
            $td
                a named piece of code, $(code:subcode&#58; name&#40;params&#41;), expanded in place at compile time with $(code:&dollar;call). See $(a:Subcode with parameters,#subcode_param).
        $tr
            $td
                block call
            $td
                a call with $(code:&amp;call), whose indented block replaces $(code:BLOCK) in the subcode. See $(a:call vs block call,#call_vs_blockcall).
        $tr
            $td
                fncode
            $td
                a function in the target language, $(code:fncode&#58; name&#40;params&#41;), called at run time. See $(a:fncode,#syntax_fncode).
        $tr
            $td
                macro
            $td
                a named text value, defined under $(code:macros&#58;) or with $(code:&dollar;&#40;set&#58;...&#41;), used as $(code:&dollar;&#40;name&#41;). See $(a:Dynamic inline macros,#dynamic_macro).
        $tr
            $td
                frame
            $td
                a subcode that wraps the page code, named as $(code:page&#58; t&#44; frame) or $(code:basic_frame) by default; it calls $(code:&dollar;call @main) where the page code goes
        $tr
            $td
                named block
            $td
                a block of output with a name, filled with $(code:&dollar;&#40;block&#58;name&#41;) or by a module
        $tr
            $td
                DUMP_STUB
            $td
                the line $(code:DUMP_STUB name), which puts out a named block where it stands; the block can be filled after the line is written
